{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgGAu6QbTyJEbWJPkEUky","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I couldn't run any experiments, so I have no result and haven't submitted a verdict. Every Bash call was denied, including a plain `python3 query.py describe`. The session is in \"don't ask\" mode, which blocks Bash, and `query.py` is the only way to reach the server, so I can't explore the tasks, train, test or submit.\n\n**Plan once Bash is allowed:**\n\n1. **Explore for free.** Run `describe` and use `compare` across many task pairs to see how much tasks overlap in behaviour. This also shows how far apart two tasks can be on input-output overlap while sharing the same rules.\n2. **Train on a few tasks** chosen so their rules are covered on purpose, for example `L1-S1-M1`, `L2-S2-M2` and `L3-S3-M3`.\n3. **Test two kinds of new task, several times each:**\n   - **Recombined tasks:** every rule was seen in training, but never in this combination (e.g. `L1-S2-M3`). I'd pick ones whose input-output overlap with the trained tasks is low.\n   - **Tasks with new rules:** at least one rule never trained (e.g. `L4-…`). I'd pick ones whose overlap with the trained tasks is high.\n4. **Read the result:**\n   - If recombined tasks are cheap to learn even when they look different, and tasks with new rules are expensive even when they look similar, the learner is part-based.\n   - If cost follows similarity to the trained tasks rather than how many rules are new, it's holistic.\n   - The tests are noisy, so I'd repeat each one about 4–6 times within the budget of 60.\n\nIf you allow Bash for `python3 query.py ...` in this directory, or switch out of \"don't ask\" mode, I'll run the experiment and submit the verdict."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":360,"cache_read_input_tokens":5278,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_in